Implement the batch API on the AMD backend over a worker thread pool

hipFile at bd0bc233 ships a batch API that cannot move a byte.
BatchContext::submit_operations validates the parameters, constructs
BatchOperation objects and inserts them into an outstanding_ops set -- and
stops.  No I/O is ever issued.  hipFileBatchIOGetStatus, hipFileBatchIOCancel
and hipFileBatchIODestroy each throw std::runtime_error("Not Implemented").
The failure mode is worse than an outright rejection: SetUp and Submit both
return hipFileSuccess, so a caller sees a healthy submission path and only
discovers the truth at the first status poll, having already lost track of
which buffers it thought were in flight.

This implements the three missing entry points and gives submit_operations
somewhere to send the work.

Why a thread pool, when hipFile already has an async engine

The asynchronous path (hipFileReadAsync / hipFileWriteAsync) exists and is
real, but it is capped at the speed of one CPU thread, and structurally so.
Fastpath::async_io puts the entire transfer inside a hipLaunchHostFunc
callback, which is how it honours the stream-ordering guarantee that the async
API makes.  HIP services host functions from exactly one thread per process,
across all streams.  Measured on MI300X / ROCm 7.14 with a 20-line HIP program
and no hipFile involved (docker/scripts/hostfunc-serial.hip.cpp in the tree
this patch is carried by):

    streams x ops   wall     if serial   if parallel   callback threads
    1 x 4            401 ms     400 ms        400 ms          1
    8 x 4           3205 ms    3200 ms        400 ms          1
    16 x 2          3204 ms    3200 ms        200 ms          1

Confirmed from the storage side with fio's libhipfile engine, eight jobs over
seven NVMe on one MI300X node: hipfile_mode=sync reaches 28.59 GB/s writes and
18.87 GB/s reads, while hipfile_mode=stream -- the same library, same drives,
same process -- reaches 5.05 and 2.00.  Sweeping queue depth 1 to 64 moves
stream throughput by under 2% (5.086 to 4.980 GB/s) while mean latency scales
exactly linearly, 1.65 ms to 107.6 ms.  That is a single server, and fitting
the block-size sweep gives its service time as 24 us + size / 5.7 GB/s.

A batch submission carries no ordering guarantee between its operations, so it
does not need hipLaunchHostFunc and is not subject to any of that.  The
operations go to worker threads that call the same synchronous path
hipFileRead and hipFileWrite use, and scale the way it does.

Implementation

  - BatchWorkerPool: one pool per BatchContextMap, created on first
    createContext so a process that never issues a batch operation never starts
    the threads.  One pool rather than one per context, because a caller that
    pools batch handles the way NIXL's GDS backend does would otherwise get
    num_handles * num_threads threads.  Worker count from
    HIPFILE_BATCH_THREADS, default 64 -- see the measurement in
    BatchWorkerPool::configured_threads.
  - Workers call Hip::hipInit() on entry, mirroring async_fastpath_copy: the
    HIP runtime SEGFAULTs when a hipFile read/write is the first HIP call made
    by a new thread.
  - BatchOperation gains a terminal state, a result, and a claim() that exactly
    one of run() and cancel() can win.  That is what stops hipFileBatchIOCancel
    from marking an operation cancelled while a worker is midway through its
    pread/pwrite.
  - run() goes through hipFileIo rather than reaching for a backend directly,
    so batch operations get the same backend selection, compat-mode fallback
    and errno mapping as the synchronous API and cannot quietly diverge from
    it.  The cost is one shared_lock on the driver state per operation.
  - A BatchNotify (mutex, condvar, completed count, in-flight count) is shared
    by a context and its operations via shared_ptr, so a worker finishing
    during teardown always has somewhere valid to write.  ~BatchContext cancels
    what has not started and then waits for in_flight to reach zero;
    destroyContext drops the context outside batch_mutex so that wait cannot
    deadlock against a concurrent createContext.
  - get_status waits on the condvar before taking context_mutex, never while
    holding it.  min_nr == 0 returns immediately, which is what fio's reap path
    calls with; a null timeout waits indefinitely; an expired timeout returns
    zero events and success rather than an error.

Not addressed here: hipFileDriverGetProperties is still unimplemented on the
AMD backend, so hipFileBatchIOSupported is not advertised even though it is now
true.  Reporting it means filling in the whole hipFileDriverProps_t, which is a
separate change.

Result, same fio configuration as above, 8 jobs x iodepth 16 x 1 MiB over
seven NVMe on one MI300X node (GB/s):

              write    read
    sync      28.30   18.73
    stream     4.96    2.01
    batch     29.32   47.23

Batch matches the synchronous path on writes and is 2.5x it on reads, against
6x and 23x the stream path.

Verified by docker/scripts/hipfile-batch-smoke.hip.cpp, 29 checks covering the
data round trip, partial reap, min_nr=0, expiring timeouts, cancel, capacity
overflow and destroy-under-load.  Upstream's gtest suite is updated for the two
new IBatchContext methods and the extra BatchOperation constructor argument.

