+/// @brief Thread pool that runs BatchOperations to completion.
+///
+/// The batch API is the only submission path in hipFile that is both
+/// asynchronous and free of HIP stream ordering, and that is the whole reason
+/// it is worth implementing on threads. The async path (hipFileReadAsync et al)
+/// cannot be: it promises that operations enqueued on one stream complete in
+/// stream order, so Fastpath::async_io puts the transfer inside a
+/// hipLaunchHostFunc callback -- and HIP services host functions from exactly
+/// one thread per process, across all streams. Measured on MI300X/ROCm 7.14:
+/// 32 callbacks of 100ms spread over 16 streams take 3204ms, not 200ms.
+///
+/// A batch submission carries no such ordering promise. Operations within one
+/// hipFileBatchIOSubmit are explicitly unordered, so they can go straight to
+/// worker threads calling the same synchronous pread/pwrite path that
+/// hipFileRead/hipFileWrite use, and scale the way that path does.
+///
+/// One pool is shared by every context (it lives in BatchContextMap) rather
+/// than one pool per context. A caller that pools batch handles the way NIXL's
+/// GDS backend does would otherwise get num_handles * num_threads threads.
+class BatchWorkerPool {
+public:
